Q1: It seems that when applying LIP, the learning process will contain pruning in addition to parameter update, which makes the whole process more complicated.

[A]: Thank you for mentioning your concern about the complexity of LIP. We would like to point out that interleaving parameter updating and pruning is quite standard and has been studied well in deep learning for many tasks in computer vision, natural language processing and more. And our proposed method is essentially different from pruning in those tasks technique-wise. Therefore, we would argue that the complexity of LIP is reasonable. To briefly summarize, our LIP method contains the following 4 steps (iteratively):
* Randomly initialize the dense DIP model;
* Optimize the network for iterations;
* Prune 20% of parameters (L1 unstructured pruning), and create the mask;
* Reset the remaining parameters to initializations and apply the mask to the structure.

This process helps us to obtain the sparse mask of the DIP network structure. Once we get the mask in desired sparsity, we indeed can construct one very sparse network architecture for DIP on one image, and then always reuse the constructed one for the future optimization of DIP with new input images (probably for another restoration task). And the high sparsity in that constructed network leads to sparse training in DIP and greatly saves flops (assuming the same number of training epochs), besides memory.

Q2: I do not quite understand the transfer property. What if a proper random strategy is specified? The performance can compete with that by transferring.

[Explanation to Transferability]: As mentioned in our response to your first question, we apply LIP to obtain a sparse mask from the original dense DIP model on one specific image (or a set of images in the case of multi-image LIP). This means we indeed can construct one very sparse network architecture for DIP. After the sparse subnetwork is identified, by “transferability” we mean that we will always reuse that subnetwork for the future optimization of DIP with new, unseen input images (or even for another restoration task). And the high sparsity in that constructed network leads to sparse training in DIP and greatly saves flops (assuming the same number of training epochs), besides memory.

[Random Structure]: We have analyzed the inner structures of different LIP subnetworks in Section 4 “LIP in Untrained NNs” - paragraph “Why is the LIP Subnetwork Special and Good?”. And plot the layer-wise sparsity ratio of SNIP pruned, random pruned and LIP subnetworks in Fig. 7. LIP tends to preserve weights of the earlier layers (closer to the input), while pruning the latter layers more aggressively (e.g, Fig. 7(a)). In contrast, SNIP tends to prune much more of the earlier layers compared to the latter ones. Random pruning by default prunes each layer at approximately the same ratio. Furthermore, we have added a new experiment: randomly prune the DIP model with the layer-wise sparsity ratio of the IMP method. In this way, we can check whether the “proper” random structure will work on the sparse subnetworks. The results are summarized in Fig. 15 in Appendix B. We observed that the randomly pruned subnetworks with LIP sparsity will collapse at a much earlier stage than those that are uniformly randomly pruned. They may have comparable performances when at low sparsity levels (e.g. from 0\% to 40\%.). But the performance curve will quickly fall at high sparsity levels (e.g., from 50\% to 90\%.), which also demonstrates the superior structure of LIPs.
